# CODEOWNERS — INERT UNTIL HUMAN DECISION H-1 IS MADE. READ THE NEXT 40 LINES.
#
# ==============================================================================================
# EVERY OWNER RULE IN THIS FILE IS COMMENTED OUT, ON PURPOSE. THIS FILE CURRENTLY ENFORCES NOTHING.
# ==============================================================================================
# contracts/OWNERS.md lists every GitHub handle in this project as `_TBD_` (human decision H-1, a
# Phase-0 exit condition in phases.md section 2). There is no honest way to write a working CODEOWNERS
# file without those handles, and there are two dishonest ways, both of which were rejected:
#
#   1. Invent plausible handles. A CODEOWNERS entry naming a user or team that does not exist, or that
#      has no write access to this repository, does not do what it looks like it does. Depending on the
#      repository's settings it is either IGNORED — the rule silently grants a free pass on the exact
#      paths it appears to protect — or it makes the "review required from Code Owners" condition
#      permanently unsatisfiable, blocking every PR. Both outcomes are worse than an empty file, and the
#      first is much worse, because it looks configured. (Which of the two you get depends on GitHub
#      settings that cannot be checked from here; stated as uncertain rather than asserted — rules.md
#      R-01.)
#   2. Ship an active file with placeholder handles so the GitHub UI flags them as errors. Rejected for
#      the same reason: the error is on a settings page nobody opens during a five-day build, and the
#      rules are not in force while it sits there.
#
# So the ownership map below is written out in full, correct and ready, and commented. Uncommenting it is
# a two-minute job once H-1 is decided. An inert file that says it is inert is safe; an active file that
# quietly grants a free pass is not.
#
# WHAT ENFORCES THE TWO-KEY RULE IN THE MEANTIME
# ---------------------------------------------
# rules.md R-22: contract changes need two keys. R-57: `main` is protected. Until this file is live, that
# is enforced ONLY by branch protection's "require 1 approving review" plus the PR template's declaration
# of which paths were touched — that is, by a setting and by human discipline, NOT by path-aware routing.
# The practical gap: right now ANY one teammate can approve a `contracts/` change. CODEOWNERS is what
# makes the second key have to be the RIGHT second key.
#
# TO ACTIVATE (do this as soon as H-1 is decided)
# ----------------------------------------------
#   1. Fill in the handle table in contracts/OWNERS.md. That table is the source of truth; this file is
#      the mechanical translation of it, and the two must not drift.
#   2. Create three GitHub teams and give each write access to this repository. Teams, not individual
#      handles: a teammate who leaves, changes username, or is on a train does not then block a merge,
#      and a team's membership is visible in one place. Individual handles are the fallback if the
#      account is a personal one where teams are unavailable.
#        gh api -X PUT "orgs/<ORG>/teams/sih26104-pair-a/repos/<ORG>/sih26104-voice-integrity" -f permission=push
#        # ...and the same for pair-b and pair-c
#   3. Uncomment every rule below and replace `<ORG>`.
#   4. THEN enable "Require review from Code Owners" in the branch protection rule for `main`. Not
#      before: enabling it against a file whose owners do not resolve is how a repository ends up
#      unmergeable at 2am.
#   5. Confirm it took effect by opening a throwaway PR that touches `contracts/frame_contract.md` and
#      checking that GitHub requests review from pair-a. A CODEOWNERS file that has never been observed
#      to request a review has not been tested (rules.md R-01).
#
# HOW CODEOWNERS RESOLUTION WORKS, since it decides whether the order below is right
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The LAST matching pattern wins — not the most specific, and not the union. So the general rules go
# first and the narrow ones go last. This is the opposite of .gitignore intuition and it is the single
# most common way a CODEOWNERS file ends up protecting nothing: a broad `*` rule placed at the bottom
# overrides every careful rule above it.
#
# Pair scopes, from contracts/OWNERS.md:
#   Pair A — Platform / Infra:        contracts/, gateway/, infra/, .github/workflows/
#   Pair B — AI / ML:                 ml/, scorer/, policy/calibration.json, evaluation/
#   Pair C — Integration & Audit:     pwa/, audit/, policy/policy.yaml, docs/runbooks/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default. Everything not matched below needs a Pair A review, because Pair A owns the repository
# skeleton, the CI, and the contracts. A default of "no owner" would mean an unmatched new top-level
# directory arrives with no review requirement at all.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# *                                @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pair B — AI / ML
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /ml/                             @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b
# /scorer/                         @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b
# /evaluation/                     @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pair C — Integration & Audit
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /pwa/                            @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c
# /audit/                          @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c
# /docs/runbooks/                  @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# policy/ is SPLIT between two pairs, and the split is not cosmetic.
#
# policy.yaml is the decision policy — thresholds, hysteresis, the action vocabulary. It is a product
# and integration artifact, so Pair C owns it. calibration.json is the score→confidence mapping derived
# from an evaluation run, so Pair B owns it. They are in the same directory and they change for
# completely different reasons: a threshold moves because an operator found a workflow too noisy, a
# calibration moves because a model was retrained. One owner for both would mean whoever owns it rubber-
# stamps half of what they see.
#
# Both are in the parity set (architecture.md 5.1), so either changing invalidates a recorded run.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /policy/policy.yaml              @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c
# /policy/calibration.json         @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# contracts/ — THE TWO-KEY PATHS (rules.md R-22)
#
# Listing multiple owners on one line means GitHub requires an approval from EACH listed team, which is
# exactly what the two-key rule is. contracts/OWNERS.md's per-file routing table is the source of these:
#
#   frame_contract.md    Pair A + Pair B + Pair C   — all three consume it, so all three must agree
#   voice_scorer.proto   Pair A + Pair B            — the wire format between gateway and scorer
#   openapi.yaml         Pair A + Pair C            — the wire format between gateway and pwa
#
# openapi.yaml also needs Pair B when `VersionInfo` or artifact state changes, and voice_scorer.proto
# needs Pair C when an audit field changes. Those are conditional on the CONTENT of the diff, which
# CODEOWNERS cannot express — it matches paths, not hunks. So they are NOT encoded here and remain the
# PR author's declaration in the pull request template. Named rather than left as a silent gap
# (rules.md R-52): this file implements the unconditional half of the routing table only.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /contracts/                      @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a
# /contracts/frame_contract.md     @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c
# /contracts/voice_scorer.proto    @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-b
# /contracts/openapi.yaml          @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-c

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two files that define who decides. Both need the tie-breaker's own key, because a change to the
# escalation rules must not be approvable under the escalation rules it is changing.
#
# Replace `@<ORG>/sih26104-tiebreaker` with the tie-breaker's INDIVIDUAL handle from OWNERS.md — a team
# is wrong here. The tie-breaker is one named person by design (H-1), and a two-person team would let
# either member approve, which reintroduces exactly the ambiguity the role exists to remove.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /contracts/CONTRACT_CHANGE_POLICY.md  @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<TIEBREAKER-HANDLE>
# /contracts/OWNERS.md                  @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<TIEBREAKER-HANDLE>
# /rules.md                             @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<TIEBREAKER-HANDLE>

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE COST AND CREDENTIAL PATHS — LAST, so nothing below can override them.
#
# These are the files where a mistake costs money or leaks access rather than causing a test failure:
#
#   .github/workflows/       adding a `push:` or `schedule:` trigger to deploy-runtime.yml turns every
#                            merge into GPU spend (rules.md R-29); weakening the `github.ref ==
#                            'refs/heads/main'` guard on a build job lets a fork PR push to ECR.
#   infra/                   the CDK app defines the ASG, the IAM role's trust policy, and the budget
#                            alarms (R-28..R-36, R-55).
#   .github/CODEOWNERS       this file. A change here changes who can approve changes.
#
# The cost owner (R-30, named in OWNERS.md) is added as a second required key on the workflow and infra
# paths — not because they write the most YAML, but because they are the person who pays for a mistake
# in it, and a review requirement lands best on whoever holds the consequence.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# /.github/                        @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a
# /.github/workflows/              @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<COST-OWNER-HANDLE>
# /infra/                          @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<COST-OWNER-HANDLE>
# /.github/CODEOWNERS              @<ORG>/sih26104-pair-a @<TIEBREAKER-HANDLE>
